<section xml:id="ws-mod6-study-guide">
  <title>Module 6: Companion Reading and Study Guide</title>
  <subtitle>Zorn §3.1–3.4 — Estimated time: 55 minutes</subtitle>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This guide accompanies four sections of Zorn's <em>Understanding
      Real Analysis</em>. Section 3.1 (<q>Limits of Functions</q>)
      introduces the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> definition and
      the sequential characterization of limits. Sections 3.2
      and 3.3 develop the theory of continuous functions, including the
      Intermediate Value Theorem and Extreme Value Theorem. Section 3.4
      introduces uniform continuity and the Heine&#x2013;Cantor theorem.
    </p>
    <p>
      As you read, keep in mind the central structural theme: the
      <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> definition is a direct analogue
      of the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> definition from Module 4.
      Every theorem about limits of functions has a counterpart for
      sequences, and the sequential characterization (Theorem 3.3 or
      its analogue in Zorn) is the bridge that lets you use Module 4
      tools here.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 3.1: LIMITS OF FUNCTIONS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod6-sg-31-intro">
    <title>Section 3.1: Limits of Functions (Zorn pp. 151&#x2013;164)</title>
    <p>
      Read the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> definition of
      <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</m> carefully. Compare it line by
      line with the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> definition of sequence
      convergence from Module 4. The structural parallel is the key
      takeaway from this section.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-31-1">
    <title>Parsing the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> definition of
        <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</m> symbolically. Then answer:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            What is the geometric meaning of the condition
            <m>0 \lt |x - a| \lt \delta</m>? In particular, why
            does <m>0 \lt |x - a|</m> (rather than <m>|x - a| \lt \delta</m>
            alone) appear?
          </li>
          <li>
            In the sequence definition, the condition was <q><m>n \geq N</m></q>.
            What is the analogue here, and what is the role of
            <m>\delta</m> compared to <m>N</m>?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The definition: <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</m> means
        <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0\ \exists \delta \gt 0</m> such that
        <m>0 \lt |x - a| \lt \delta \Rightarrow |f(x) - L| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) The condition <m>0 \lt |x - a| \lt \delta</m> describes a
        <em>punctured</em> open interval around <m>a</m>: <m>x</m>
        is within <m>\delta</m> of <m>a</m> but <em>not equal to</em>
        <m>a</m>. The strict inequality <m>0 \lt |x - a|</m> is
        essential because the limit concerns the behavior of <m>f</m>
        <em>near</em> <m>a</m>, not <em>at</em> <m>a</m>; in
        particular, <m>f</m> need not even be defined at <m>a</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) In Module 4, <m>N</m> is a threshold index past which
        the sequence stays within <m>\varepsilon</m> of <m>L</m>.
        Here, <m>\delta</m> plays the same role: it is a threshold
        distance from <m>a</m> within which <m>f(x)</m> stays within
        <m>\varepsilon</m> of <m>L</m>. The two definitions have
        exactly parallel logical structure.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-31-2">
    <title>A Basic <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> Verification</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove from the definition that <m>\lim_{x \to 3} (2x - 1) = 5</m>.
        Show your scratch work (finding <m>\delta</m>) separately from
        the formal proof.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <em>Scratch work.</em>
        <m>|f(x) - 5| = |2x - 1 - 5| = |2x - 6| = 2|x - 3|</m>.
        For this to be less than <m>\varepsilon</m>, we need
        <m>|x - 3| \lt \varepsilon / 2</m>. Set <m>\delta = \varepsilon / 2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Proof.</em>
        Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. Set <m>\delta = \varepsilon / 2</m>.
        Suppose <m>0 \lt |x - 3| \lt \delta</m>. Then
        <m>|f(x) - 5| = 2|x - 3| \lt 2\delta = \varepsilon</m>.
        By definition, <m>\lim_{x \to 3} (2x - 1) = 5</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-31-3">
    <title>Sequential Characterization of Limits</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's sequential characterization of limits (sometimes
        called the Heine criterion): <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</m> if
        and only if for every sequence <m>(x_n)</m> with
        <m>x_n \to a</m> and <m>x_n \neq a</m> for all <m>n</m>, we
        have <m>f(x_n) \to L</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Explain in one sentence how this theorem connects Module 4
            to Module 6.
          </li>
          <li>
            Use the sequential characterization to argue that
            <m>\lim_{x \to 0} \sin(1/x)</m> does not exist. (Hint:
            find two sequences approaching 0 along which the function
            values approach different values.)
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) The sequential characterization reduces questions about
        function limits to questions about sequence limits, so every
        Module 4 tool (Algebra of Limits, Squeeze Theorem, etc.) applies
        directly to function limits via this bridge.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Let <m>x_n = 1/(2\pi n)</m> and <m>y_n = 1/(\pi/2 + 2\pi n)</m>.
        Both satisfy <m>x_n \to 0</m> and <m>y_n \to 0</m> with
        <m>x_n \neq 0</m> and <m>y_n \neq 0</m>. We have
        <m>\sin(1/x_n) = \sin(2\pi n) = 0 \to 0</m> and
        <m>\sin(1/y_n) = \sin(\pi/2 + 2\pi n) = 1 \to 1</m>.
        Since two sequences approaching 0 give different function
        values, the sequential characterization implies the limit does
        not exist.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTIONS 3.2–3.4: CONTINUITY AND UNIFORM CONTINUITY
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod6-sg-32-intro">
    <title>Sections 3.2&#x2013;3.4: Continuity and Uniform Continuity (Zorn pp. 164&#x2013;198)</title>
    <p>
      Section 3.2 defines continuity at a point and on a set, and
      develops the algebra of continuous functions. Section 3.3 proves
      the Intermediate Value Theorem and Extreme Value Theorem. Section
      3.4 introduces uniform continuity and the Heine&#x2013;Cantor
      theorem. Read all three before working the exercises below.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-32-1">
    <title>Continuity at a Point</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> definition of
        continuity of <m>f</m> at <m>a</m>. Then prove that
        <m>f(x) = x^2</m> is continuous at <m>a = 3</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (Hint: <m>|x^2 - 9| = |x - 3||x + 3|</m>. Restrict
        <m>|x - 3| \lt 1</m> first to bound <m>|x + 3|</m>, then set
        <m>\delta = \min(1, \varepsilon / 7)</m>.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <em>Definition.</em>
        <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> if <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0\
        \exists \delta \gt 0</m> such that <m>|x - a| \lt \delta \Rightarrow
        |f(x) - f(a)| \lt \varepsilon</m>. (Note: unlike the limit definition,
        we do not need <m>0 \lt |x - a|</m>, because continuity
        includes the requirement that <m>f(a)</m> is the right value.)
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Scratch work.</em>
        <m>|x^2 - 9| = |x - 3||x + 3|</m>. If <m>|x - 3| \lt 1</m>
        then <m>|x| \lt 4</m>, so <m>|x + 3| \leq |x| + 3 \lt 7</m>.
        So <m>|x^2 - 9| \lt 7|x - 3|</m>. Set <m>\delta = \min(1, \varepsilon/7)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Proof.</em>
        Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. Set <m>\delta = \min(1, \varepsilon/7)</m>.
        Suppose <m>|x - 3| \lt \delta</m>. Since <m>\delta \leq 1</m>,
        we have <m>|x - 3| \lt 1</m>, so <m>|x + 3| \lt 7</m>.
        Thus <m>|x^2 - 9| = |x - 3||x + 3| \lt \delta \cdot 7 \leq \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-32-2">
    <title>Intermediate Value Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT). Then use it to
        show that the equation <m>x^3 + x - 1 = 0</m> has at least
        one solution in the interval <m>(0, 1)</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Identify the function <m>f</m> and verify it is continuous
            on <m>[0, 1]</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Compute <m>f(0)</m> and <m>f(1)</m> and verify they have
            opposite signs.
          </li>
          <li>
            Invoke the IVT by name to conclude.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <em>IVT.</em>
        If <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a, b]</m> and <m>f(a) \lt 0 \lt f(b)</m>
        (or <m>f(a) \gt 0 \gt f(b)</m>), then there exists <m>c \in (a, b)</m>
        with <m>f(c) = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) Let <m>f(x) = x^3 + x - 1</m>. As a polynomial, <m>f</m>
        is continuous everywhere, hence on <m>[0, 1]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>f(0) = 0 + 0 - 1 = -1 \lt 0</m> and
        <m>f(1) = 1 + 1 - 1 = 1 \gt 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Since <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[0, 1]</m> with
        <m>f(0) \lt 0 \lt f(1)</m>, the Intermediate Value Theorem
        guarantees a <m>c \in (0, 1)</m> with <m>f(c) = 0</m>, i.e.,
        <m>c^3 + c - 1 = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-32-3">
    <title>Extreme Value Theorem and Compactness</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the Extreme Value Theorem (EVT). Then give an example
        showing the EVT can fail if the interval is not closed and
        bounded.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Let <m>f(x) = 1/x</m> on <m>(0, 1]</m>. Does <m>f</m>
            attain a maximum value? Does it attain a minimum?
          </li>
          <li>
            What hypothesis of the EVT fails for this example?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <em>EVT.</em>
        If <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a, b]</m> (a closed bounded
        interval), then there exist <m>c, d \in [a, b]</m> with
        <m>f(c) \leq f(x) \leq f(d)</m> for all <m>x \in [a, b]</m>.
        That is, <m>f</m> attains both its minimum and maximum values.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) <m>f(x) = 1/x</m> on <m>(0, 1]</m> has minimum value
        <m>f(1) = 1</m> (attained) but no maximum: as
        <m>x \to 0^+</m>, <m>f(x) \to +\infty</m>, so <m>f</m> is
        unbounded above.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) The interval <m>(0, 1]</m> is not closed (it does not
        contain its left endpoint 0), violating the hypothesis of the
        EVT.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-34-1">
    <title>Definition of Uniform Continuity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the definition of uniform continuity of <m>f</m> on a
        set <m>S</m>. Then prove that <m>f(x) = 2x + 1</m> is
        uniformly continuous on <m>\R</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Identify the key difference in quantifier order between
            the definition of pointwise continuity on <m>S</m> and
            the definition of uniform continuity on <m>S</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Prove uniform continuity of <m>f(x) = 2x + 1</m> on
            <m>\R</m> by finding <m>\delta</m> in terms of
            <m>\varepsilon</m> only (not depending on any point
            <m>x</m> or <m>y</m>).
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <em>Definition.</em>
        <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>S</m> if
        <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0\ \exists \delta \gt 0</m> such that
        for all <m>x, y \in S</m> with <m>|x - y| \lt \delta</m>,
        we have <m>|f(x) - f(y)| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) In pointwise continuity on <m>S</m>, the definition reads:
        for all <m>x \in S</m>, for all <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, there
        exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m> (which may depend on both <m>\varepsilon</m>
        and <m>x</m>) such that <m>|y - x| \lt \delta \Rightarrow
        |f(y) - f(x)| \lt \varepsilon</m>. In uniform continuity,
        the quantifier <m>\forall x \in S</m> is moved inside the
        <m>\exists \delta</m>: for all <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, there
        exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m> (depending only on <m>\varepsilon</m>)
        such that for <em>all</em> <m>x, y \in S</m> with
        <m>|x - y| \lt \delta</m>, we have <m>|f(x) - f(y)| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>|f(x) - f(y)| = |(2x + 1) - (2y + 1)| = 2|x - y|</m>.
        Given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, set <m>\delta = \varepsilon / 2</m>.
        Then for any <m>x, y \in \R</m> with <m>|x - y| \lt \delta</m>,
        we get <m>|f(x) - f(y)| = 2|x - y| \lt 2\delta = \varepsilon</m>.
        The same <m>\delta</m> works for all <m>x, y</m>, so <m>f</m>
        is uniformly continuous.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-34-2">
    <title>Failure of Uniform Continuity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that <m>f(x) = 1/x</m> is <em>not</em> uniformly
        continuous on <m>(0, 1)</m>. Use the sequential criterion for
        non-uniform continuity: find sequences <m>(x_n)</m> and
        <m>(y_n)</m> in <m>(0, 1)</m> with <m>|x_n - y_n| \to 0</m>
        but <m>|f(x_n) - f(y_n)| \not\to 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Let <m>x_n = 1/n</m> and <m>y_n = 1/(n + 1)</m>. Both lie in
        <m>(0, 1)</m> for <m>n \geq 2</m>. We have
        <me>|x_n - y_n| = \frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n+1} = \frac{1}{n(n+1)} \to 0.</me>
        But
        <me>|f(x_n) - f(y_n)| = |n - (n+1)| = 1</me>
        for all <m>n</m>. Since <m>|f(x_n) - f(y_n)| = 1 \not\to 0</m>,
        the definition of uniform continuity fails for <m>\varepsilon = 1/2</m>
        (say): no single <m>\delta</m> can prevent <m>|f(x) - f(y)|</m>
        from exceeding <m>1/2</m> for pairs <m>x, y</m> near 0.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod6-sg-34-3">
    <title>Heine&#x2013;Cantor Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Zorn's statement of the Heine&#x2013;Cantor theorem: if
        <m>f</m> is continuous on a closed bounded interval <m>[a, b]</m>,
        then <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>[a, b]</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Why does the theorem require <em>closed and bounded</em>
            rather than just bounded? (Think about what fails for
            <m>f(x) = 1/x</m> on <m>(0, 1]</m>.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Why does the theorem require <em>continuity on all of</em>
            <m>[a, b]</m> rather than just continuity at each
            interior point?
          </li>
          <li>
            The proof of Heine&#x2013;Cantor uses the theory of
            compact sets and open covers. Zorn may prove it differently.
            Identify the key property of <m>[a, b]</m> that the proof
            uses, and note that this is a topic developed fully in
            MAT 5610.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>f(x) = 1/x</m> is continuous on <m>(0, 1]</m> but not
        uniformly continuous (Exercise sg-34-2): the interval is bounded
        but not closed (0 is missing), and the function blows up
        near the missing endpoint.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) If continuity fails at even one point in <m>[a, b]</m>,
        the function may have a jump discontinuity, which would
        prevent uniform continuity near that point.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) The key property is <em>compactness</em> of <m>[a, b]</m>:
        every open cover has a finite subcover (Heine&#x2013;Borel
        theorem). The proof extracts a finite cover from the family of
        <m>\delta</m>-neighborhoods that work pointwise, then takes
        the minimum of the finitely many <m>\delta</m> values.
        Compactness is developed rigorously in MAT 5610.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

</section>
